# 14 — Hydrostatic equilibrium: why pressure grows with depth
#
# Any layer of a star (or an atmosphere) that isn't moving must be held up:
# the pressure below it pushes up exactly as hard as gravity pulls it down.
# For a thin shell at radius r this balance reads
#
#     dP/dr = −G M(r) ρ(r) / r²,        dM/dr = 4π r² ρ(r)
#
# where M(r) is the mass inside radius r. Part 1 builds a star of constant
# density, where the exact central pressure is P_c = 3 G M² / (8π R⁴).
# Part 2 applies the same idea to the Earth's atmosphere at constant
# temperature, where the pressure falls off exponentially with a
# "scale height" H = R_gas T / (M_air g).

# Part 1: a Sun of uniform density
ρ0 = M_sun / ((4/3) π R_sun³)
r_start = 1 km            # start just off-centre: G M / r² is 0/0 at r = 0
solve P' = -G M ρ0 / r²,
      M' = 4π r² ρ0
  with P(r_start) = 0 Pa, M(r_start) = (4/3) π r_start³ ρ0
  for r from r_start to R_sun

# We integrated with P = 0 at the centre, so the central pressure is minus
# the value we reach at the surface.
P_c = -P(R_sun)
print "mean density of the Sun:", ρ0 in g/cm³ to 3 digits
print "central pressure (solve):  ", P_c to 5 digits
print "central pressure (formula):", 3 G M_sun² / (8π R_sun⁴) to 5 digits
print "mass reached at the surface / M_sun:", M(R_sun) / M_sun to 6 digits
print "in atmospheres:", P_c in atm to 3 digits

# Temperature from the ideal gas law P = ρ k_B T / (μ m_p), with μ ≈ 0.6 for
# ionised solar gas: a first estimate of the Sun's core temperature
μ = 0.6
T_c = P_c μ m_p / (ρ0 k_B)
print "estimated central temperature:", T_c to 2 digits, " (real: 1.6×10⁷ K)"

# Part 2: an isothermal atmosphere, dP/dz = −ρ g with ρ = P M_air / (R_gas T)
M_air = 28.97 g/mol
T_air = 288 K
solve p' = -p M_air g_n / (R_gas T_air)
  with p(0) = 1 atm
  for z from 0 km to 30 km

# (called H_atm: in `8849 m / H` the H would be read as the unit henry)
H_atm = R_gas T_air / (M_air g_n)
print "scale height H =", H_atm in km
print "pressure at Everest's summit (8849 m):", p(8849 m) in kPa, "  exact:", 101.325 kPa * exp(-8849 m / H_atm)
print "pressure at 30 km:", p(30 km) in kPa

plot p in kPa vs z in km to "gallery/atmosphere.png"
